That's right Pete, this happens because `<tr>` cannot be used on their own. They need to be inside of a `table` element.

We use DOMPurify to sanitize our HTML, and if we wanted to allow `tr` tags on their own, we could look into implementing a DOMPurify hook. I would be very careful about that because we don't want to change the sanitization behavior and break apps or introduce security issues. Here's a DOMPurify bug report on using dom snippets with DOMPurify. [Can't sanitize some DOM snippets, e.g. <td>text</td> #190
 ](https://github.com/cure53/DOMPurify/issues/190)
 
@kvetis , here' a work-around to try 🤞 

Wrap the innerHtml in a `table` element styled as `display:contents;`
```<table [innerHTML]="'<table style=\'display:contents;\'><tr><td>Hello</td><td>World</td></tr></table>'"></table>```
